Q: What is the benefit of another API useEffectEvent over old useRef? A: <p>The comparison isn't correct because these are different ways to achieve the same goal, and <code>refIncrement</code> is relatively ugly workaround for that.</p>
<p>A more closer solution would be:</p>
<pre><code>...
const onTickRef = ref();
onTickRef.current = () =&gt; setCount(c =&gt; c + increment);
...
onTickRef.current();
</code></pre>
<p>This is <a href="https://github.com/sanity-io/use-effect-event/blob/v2.0.3/src/useEffectEvent.ts#L24-L51" rel="nofollow noreferrer">accurate <code>useEventEffect</code> ponyfill</a> that shows that it does roughly the same, but with a guard that prevents calling a function during render and without the need to access <code>current</code> to unwrap function ref.</p>

Q: C program gets stuck while executing A: <p>This is issued by the <code>libpulsecommon-15.99.so</code> library. <code>SDL_Init</code> initializes the <strong>Simple DirectMedia Layer (SDL)</strong> library (commonly SDL2). I assume <code>SDL_INIT_EVERYTHING</code> includes <code>SDL_INIT_AUDIO</code> which is probably trying to invoke the <code>libpulsecommon</code> library. <code>libpulsecommon</code> uses a cookie to talk to its daemon, and the cookie path may erroneously fall on a special filesystem that doesn't support the <code>ftruncate</code> call (is your home directory a network filesystem, a FUSE filesystem, a read-only filesystem, or something other than a normal read/writable <code>extNfs</code>-equivalent filesystem?).</p>
<p>You're also doing math (<code>header[4]*16*1024</code>) with a signed char (<code>char header[16];</code>) which if I am not mistaken is a <em>very bad</em> idea. What could happen if that header element is over 127, it will become negative, which will make the math a very large negative number, which, depending on how <code>size</code> is defined and/or used, <em>could</em> turn into a extremely large sign-extended unsigned value (i.e., a 128 could become a size of <code>0xffffffffffffffff</code>), which could definitely make funky stuff happen:</p>
<pre><code>13:37:07:~/src$ gcc -o signed_char signed_char.c
13:37:09:~/src$ cat signed_char.c
#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdint.h&gt;

int main() {
  char header = 0x80;
  size_t size;

  size = header * 16 * 1024;
  printf (&quot;%zu\n&quot;, size);
}
13:37:22:~/src$ ./signed_char
18446744073707454464
</code></pre>
